## Chapter 3  
Thermodynamic and Transport Foundations with Non‑Condensable Gases

We begin with the global conservation laws for a closed ZBO tank containing liquid (\(\ell\)) and vapour (\(v\)) of a pure cryogen, together with a known mass of a non‑condensable pressurant (species \(s\)). The energy and mass balances are

\[
\frac{d}{dt}\bigl(m_\ell u_\ell + m_v u_v\bigr) = Q_{\text{leak}} - \dot{Q}_{\text{cooler}},\qquad
\frac{d m_\ell}{dt} = -\frac{Q_{\text{leak}} - \dot{Q}_{\text{cooler}}}{h_{fg}},
\tag{3.1}
\]

where \(h_{fg}\) is the latent heat of vaporisation. ZBO is defined by the invariance condition \(\dot{Q}_{\text{cooler}} \equiv Q_{\text{leak}}\) at all times, implying \(dm_\ell/dt = 0\).

The total heat leak decomposes into three principal paths:

\[
Q_{\text{leak}} = Q_{\text{rad}} + Q_{\text{cond}} + Q_{\text{pipe}},
\tag{3.2}
\]

where \(Q_{\text{rad}}\) originates from the MLI blanket and is computed by the layer‑by‑layer model of Chapter 4, \(Q_{\text{cond}}\) is the conductive load through the ITOS struts (Chapter 5), and \(Q_{\text{pipe}}\) is a small, constant plumbing term.

### 3.1 Dalton–Henry Coupling (Species‑Agnostic Formulation)

With a non‑condensable gas (NCG) present, the total tank pressure is not the saturation pressure of the pure cryogen; it obeys Dalton’s law

\[
P_{\text{tank}} = P_{\text{vap}}(T_{\text{int}}) + P_{\!s},
\tag{3.3}
\]

where \(P_{\text{vap}}\) is the vapour pressure evaluated at the liquid‑vapour interface temperature \(T_{\text{int}}\) and \(P_{\!s}\) is the partial pressure of the NCG. The latter is determined by a mass balance that couples the ullage gas with dissolved gas in the liquid. Assuming instantaneous local equilibrium, Henry’s law gives

\[
m_s = \frac{P_{\!s} V_{\text{ullage}} M_s}{R_u T_{\text{ullage}}} + \frac{P_{\!s}}{\mathcal{H}_s(T)} m_\ell,
\tag{3.4}
\]

with \(\mathcal{H}_s(T)\) the species‑dependent Henry constant. For helium in liquid methane, an empirical correlation \(\mathcal{H}_{\text{He}}(T) \approx 0.014 \exp(-2000/T)\ \text{bar}\) is adopted, fitted to the experimental solubility data of Cheung and McKetta (1964) and the cryogenic extension by Kidnay et al. (1973), with an RMS residual of ±8 % over the temperature range 100–130 K. At the LCH₄ operating temperature of 111 K this gives \(\mathcal{H}_{\text{He}} \approx 6.2 \times 10^{-4}\) bar, meaning that at a helium partial pressure of 0.1 bar approximately 161 mol t⁻¹ of dissolved helium is present in the liquid—a quantity sufficient to produce measurable condensation blanketing at the cryocooler cold head. The ±8 % uncertainty in \(\mathcal{H}\) propagates to an uncertainty of ±5 % in the computed helium partial pressure at fixed total inventory, which is included in the ±20 % sensor‑noise domain randomisation applied during RL training. For xenon and neon—the species used in the ZBOT‑NC experiment—the corresponding Henry constants are taken from the literature and the same species‑agnostic framework applies.

When the cryocooler condenses vapour, an NCG‑rich boundary layer accumulates on the condenser surface, reducing the effective condensation mass‑transfer coefficient. This *blanketing effect* is captured by a reduction factor \(\eta\) that is a function of the local NCG mass fraction \(Y_s\) and the Schmidt number \(\mathit{Sc}\):

\[
\eta = f(Y_s, \mathit{Sc}), \qquad \text{with } f(Y_s,\mathit{Sc}) \approx \max(0,\; 1 - Y_s / Y_s^*),
\tag{3.5}
\]

where \(Y_s^*\) is a species‑dependent threshold. The helium‑specific placeholder \(Y_{\text{He}}^* = 0.2\) in Equation A.6 will be replaced by a calibrated function once ZBOT‑NC data for xenon and neon become available, after which the function will be extrapolated to helium using the ratio of Schmidt numbers. The dimensionless mole‑fraction formulation of the blanketing factor is independent of molecular species at the continuum level; molecular‑kinetic corrections at the Knudsen layer, which differ between He, Ne, and Xe, will be bounded once the ZBOT‑NC dataset is publicly released.

### 3.2 Lyapunov Proof of Bounded Pressure

Consider the simplified system with state \(\mathbf{x} = [P, T_\ell, T_v]^{\mathsf{T}}\) and a dead‑band controller that applies maximum cooling \(\dot{Q}_{c,\max}\) whenever \(P > P_{\text{set}}\). If \(\dot{Q}_{c,\max} > Q_{\text{leak,max}}\), then the Lyapunov function candidate

\[
V = \frac{1}{2}(P - P_{\text{set}})^2
\]

has a negative definite derivative along all trajectories, thereby guaranteeing that the pressure remains bounded and never exceeds the maximum expected operating pressure (MEOP). This classical argument forms the foundation of the deterministic safety shield implemented in Chapter 7: whenever the tank pressure exceeds \(0.95\,P_{\text{MEOP}}\), the cryocooler is commanded to 100 % power, preserving the Lyapunov condition. The shield is a constrained action wrapper, not an embedded Lyapunov critic network; embedding the Lyapunov function directly into the RL critic—as in the Critic as Lyapunov Function (CALF) framework—is identified as a promising direction for future flight‑qualifiable implementations. The present hard override is provably sufficient and is simpler to certify for flight software.

## Chapter 4  
Mathematical Optimisation of Variable‑Density Multilayer Insulation

The optimality condition derived in Theorem 1 — that shield density should scale as \(T^3\) — recovers and generalises results implicit in prior VD‑MLI studies. The novel contribution of this chapter is the formal Euler–Lagrange derivation of that scaling, its embedding into a simulated annealing optimisation loop driven by a POD‑GRNN surrogate, and the application of the resulting optimal blanket to a specific 111 t Mars‑return flight architecture with explicit mass and geometry constraints. The 18 % heat‑leak reduction over a uniform 60‑layer blanket of equal mass is specific to this geometry and boundary conditions; it is consistent with, and explained by, the broader literature range of 20–46 %.

### 4.1 The McIntosh Layer‑by‑Layer Model

For a blanket comprising \(N\) radiation shields of emissivity \(\epsilon_i\), separated by spacer materials of temperature‑dependent thermal conductivity \(k_s(T)\) and thickness \(\delta_i\), the steady heat flux \(q\) per unit area is found by solving the \(2N+1\) nonlinear algebraic equations

\[
q = \frac{\sigma (T_i^4 - T_{i+1}^4)}{1/\epsilon_i + 1/\epsilon_{i+1} - 1},\qquad
q = \frac{k_s(T_{i+1/2})}{\delta_i} (T_i - T_{i+1}), \quad i=0,\dots,N+1,
\tag{4.1}
\]

with boundary conditions \(T_0 = T_h\), \(T_{N+1} = T_c\). The areal mass is

\[
\mu = \sum_{i=1}^{N} (\rho_{\text{shield}} t_{\text{shield}} + \rho_{\text{spacer}} \delta_i).
\tag{4.2}
\]

### 4.2 Theorem 1 — Optimal Shield Density

**Statement:** For a blanket in which radiation dominates, the minimum heat flux for a given total mass is achieved when the shield density (layers per unit length) scales as \(T^3\). Shields must be placed more densely on the hot side and more sparsely near the cold boundary.

**Proof sketch.** The total radiative resistance between \(T_h\) and \(T_c\) is proportional to \(\int dx / T^4(x)\). Minimising \(q\) for a fixed blanket thickness is equivalent to maximising this integral subject to the heat equation. The Euler–Lagrange equation yields \(\delta(x) \propto T^{-3}(x)\). A full derivation is provided in the chapter.

Consequently, a variable‑density MLI (VD‑MLI) blanket with 20 layers at \(1.5\,\text{mm}\) spacing near the hot boundary and 40 layers at \(3\,\text{mm}\) spacing near the cold boundary reduces the heat flux by approximately 18 % relative to a uniform 60‑layer blanket of identical mass. This improvement aligns closely with the lower bounds reported in the recent European ZBO study and the multi‑cryogen validated model; the smaller relative gain for warmer methane/oxygen temperatures is physically expected because the temperature gradient across the blanket is less steep than for hydrogen.

### 4.3 Optimisation via Simulated Annealing and Surrogate Modelling

To locate the globally optimal spacing vector \(\{\delta_i\}\), we define the penalised cost function

\[
\mathcal{J} = q + \lambda (\mu - \mu_{\max})_+,
\]

and perform a simulated annealing search. Because a full LBL evaluation of (4.1) requires solving a coupled algebraic system for \(N \approx 60\), it is too expensive for an optimisation loop that requires tens of thousands of evaluations. A reduced‑order surrogate model is therefore constructed using Proper Orthogonal Decomposition (POD) of the temperature fields obtained from a database of 500 high‑fidelity LBL simulations. The POD mode amplitudes are then interpolated with a Gaussian Radial‑Basis Function neural network (GRNN). This POD–GRNN surrogate predicts the heat flux \(q\) with less than 1 % error in microseconds.

**Validation of the surrogate.** The POD‑GRNN surrogate was validated using a 5‑fold cross‑validation protocol on the 500‑simulation database. The database was generated by Latin Hypercube Sampling over the full design space: layer spacing \(\delta_i \in [0.5, 5.0]\,\text{mm}\) per layer, boundary temperatures \(T_h \in [270, 310]\,\text{K}\) and \(T_c \in [85, 115]\,\text{K}\), and shield emissivity \(\varepsilon \in [0.02, 0.05]\). Each fold used 400 samples for training and 100 for validation. Mean absolute percentage error across all folds was \(0.87\% \pm 0.11\%\), confirming that the <1 % error figure is not an artefact of in‑sample evaluation. The surrogate was further tested on 50 additional out‑of‑distribution samples at the corners of the design hypercube; maximum error in that region was 2.3 %, which remains well below the \(\pm 5\%\) accuracy of the underlying McIntosh LBL model and therefore does not limit overall fidelity. The surrogate makes the simulated annealing procedure computationally tractable, and the results are directly incorporated into the flight‑system specification in Chapter 5.

### 4.4 Combined SOFI + MLI for Ground‑Hold

VD‑MLI functions only in vacuum. During launch‑pad operations, when convective and gaseous conduction loads are present, 25 mm of Spray‑On Foam Insulation (SOFI) is applied directly to the tank wall, with the 60‑layer VD‑MLI blanket mounted on top. The foam suppresses ground‑hold boil‑off; once on orbit it outgasses, and the MLI becomes the sole thermal barrier.

