## Chapter 7  
Autonomous Cryo‑Management via Reinforcement Learning — Proposed Architecture

This chapter presents the design of the autonomous control layer. The RL agent, the safety shield, the reward structure, and the training strategy are all described as a blueprint. No actual training or ablation studies have been conducted; the quantitative comparisons with model‑predictive control are *projected* based on the structural properties of the two control paradigms.

### 7.1 Control Problem Formulation

The RL agent selects the cryocooler compressor power \(u \in [0, P_{\text{max}}]\) once per minute. The objective is encoded in the reward function

\[
r = -w_1 \dot{m}_{\text{vent}}^2 - w_2 u + w_3 \mathbf{1}_{P < P_{\text{set}}},
\tag{7.1}
\]

where the weights \(w_1\), \(w_2\), and \(w_3\) balance the conflicting goals of minimising vent losses, conserving electrical power, and maintaining a safe pressure margin. The numerical values \(w_1 = 10^{-8}\), \(w_2 = 10^{-3}\), and \(w_3 = 0.5\) are chosen so that all three terms are dimensionally comparable at the expected operating point (vent mass flow ~\(10^{-4}\,\text{kg s}^{-1}\), cooler power range 0–380 W). Their final tuning will be part of the training campaign; a planned ablation study across a range of weight vectors will verify that the learned policy is qualitatively robust to the precise balance of objectives.

The state vector available to the agent is 14‑dimensional: tank pressure, liquid and ullage temperatures, helium partial pressure, solar irradiance, available electrical power, cold‑head temperature, and time to the next anticipated thermal disturbance.

### 7.2 Safety Shield with Lyapunov Guarantee

A deterministic safety shield is placed around the RL policy. Whenever the measured tank pressure exceeds \(0.95\,P_{\text{MEOP}}\), the cryocooler is commanded to maximum power irrespective of the agent’s output. The Lyapunov analysis of Chapter 3 proves that this override renders the closed‑loop system Input‑to‑State Stable (ISS) with respect to bounded heat‑leak disturbances, guaranteeing that pressure never exceeds MEOP. The shield is a simple, certifiable wrapper; it does not require the RL critic network to itself encode a Lyapunov function. The more integrated Critic as Lyapunov Function (CALF) methodology is noted as a candidate for future refinement, but the hard override is considered sufficient for the present architecture.

### 7.3 Algorithm Selection: Soft Actor‑Critic

The chosen RL algorithm is Soft Actor‑Critic (SAC), an off‑policy maximum‑entropy method well‑suited to continuous control problems with high‑dimensional state spaces. The entropy bonus encourages exploration and prevents premature convergence to brittle policies. The planned training setup includes:

- Twin Q‑networks with 512‑512‑256‑128 fully connected layers and layer normalisation.
- A stochastic policy network of similar depth, outputting the mean and standard deviation of a Gaussian action distribution.
- A replay buffer of size \(2 \times 10^6\) transitions.
- Discount factor \(\gamma = 0.99\).
- Soft target network updates with \(\tau = 0.005\).

Training will be conducted on the reduced‑order digital twin (Chapter 6) for one million episodes, with extensive domain randomisation applied to the digital twin’s parameters: heat flux \(\pm 30\%\), sensor noise \(\pm 20\%\), and random slosh events. The accommodation coefficient uncertainty (\(\pm 18\%\) around the calibrated value) and the Henry’s Law constant uncertainty (\(\pm 5\%\)) will be subsumed into this randomisation envelope.

Once training converges, the policy will be distilled into a compact feed‑forward network suitable for deployment on a flight‑qualified processor. This entire training and distillation pipeline is described as the intended methodology; no training runs have yet been executed.

### 7.4 Projected Advantage over Model‑Predictive Control

A linearised Model‑Predictive Controller (MPC) serves as the baseline against which the RL agent will be compared. The MPC minimises a quadratic cost over a finite prediction horizon, using a linear state‑space model of the tank thermodynamics. Under nominal, steady‑state conditions, both controllers are expected to maintain ZBO.

The structural limitation of the linear MPC is its inability to anticipate transient disturbances that lie beyond its prediction horizon or are not captured by its linear model. The SAC agent, by learning directly from the high‑fidelity digital twin, is expected to develop anticipatory pre‑cooling strategies that begin to counteract a heat‑flux spike before the pressure rise becomes critical. Preliminary simulation experiments reported in the literature for analogous storage systems suggest that such strategies can yield a cumulative reward improvement on the order of 20–30 % under unmodelled disturbances. It is therefore **projected** that the SAC agent will outperform the linear MPC by approximately 22 % in cumulative reward when subjected to a 30 % heat‑flux spike. This figure is a target, derived from the structural advantages of learned pre‑emptive control, and will be verified once the planned training campaign is complete. A thorough ablation study over the reward weights will accompany that campaign, as described in Section 7.1, to ensure the result is not an artefact of a particular reward parameterisation.

### 7.5 Originality and Contribution

To the best of the author’s knowledge, following a systematic search of the NASA Technical Reports Server, arXiv, and the proceedings of the AIAA Propulsion and Energy Forum through May 2025, no prior publication proposes a fully specified, safety‑shielded reinforcement‑learning controller for a zero‑boil‑off cryogenic tank that integrates a microgravity‑validated digital twin, a non‑condensable gas thermodynamic model, and a Lyapunov‑based safety proof. The present contribution is therefore characterised as the first complete architectural blueprint for such a system, ready for computational implementation and, subsequently, experimental validation.

## Chapter 8  
Validation Strategy Without Laboratory Experiments

This dissertation is theoretical and architectural; no physical calorimeter, injection test, sub‑scale tank, or reinforcement‑learning training run has been constructed or executed. All validation is therefore proposed against existing and future public flight datasets and through established numerical verification protocols. The strategy is designed to demonstrate mission readiness at the architectural level, leaving a follow‑on programme of computational and sub‑scale experimental work for flight qualification.

### 8.1 Tier 1 — Pure‑Fluid Physics (ZBOT‑1)

The ZBOT‑1 experiment, which collected long‑duration microgravity pressure‑rise data on the ISS during 2017–2018 using the simulant fluid perfluoro‑n‑pentane (PnP), provides the primary validation target for the pure‑fluid components of the digital twin. The VOF‑Schrage model described in Chapter 6 will be calibrated against the publicly available ZBOT‑1 pressure‑rise curves by adjusting the accommodation coefficient \(\hat{\sigma}\) within the physically plausible range \(10^{-5}\) to \(10^{-1}\). Published CFD studies employing the same numerical framework have reported agreement within \(\pm 7\%\) with these experimental curves; the present twin is expected to achieve a comparable level of fidelity once the calibration is performed. The calibration has not yet been carried out, and the \(\pm 7\%\) figure is therefore a target, not a demonstrated result. A systematic sensitivity analysis across three decades of \(\hat{\sigma}\) is planned to quantify the dependence of the pressure‑rise prediction on this parameter, and the resulting uncertainty envelope will be incorporated into the domain randomisation strategy for the reinforcement‑learning training campaign.

### 8.2 Tier 2 — Non‑Condensable Gas Physics (ZBOT‑NC)

ZBOT‑NC launched to the ISS in September 2025 and is currently collecting microgravity data with xenon and neon as the non‑condensable surrogates. The Dalton–Henry coupling model of Appendix A.3 will be validated against the released dataset using a cross‑species mapping based on the dimensionless mole‑fraction blanketing factor, which is independent of molecular species at the continuum level. Molecular‑kinetic corrections specific to helium will be bounded using the existing He–LH₂ ground‑test literature. This validation pathway is pre‑structured in the model and requires no architectural change; it will be executed as soon as the ZBOT‑NC data are made publicly available.

### 8.3 Tier 3 — Insulation Model Verification

The McIntosh layer‑by‑layer (LBL) model is verified against published cryostat heat‑leak measurements from McIntosh’s original 1996 study, covering boundary temperatures down to 77 K. The agreement between the LBL predictions and the experimental data is within \(\pm 5\%\) for all documented cases. The POD‑GRNN surrogate constructed in Chapter 4 was validated against the LBL model using a 5‑fold cross‑validation protocol on a Latin Hypercube sample of 500 simulations, achieving a mean absolute percentage error of \(0.87\% \pm 0.11\%\). Maximum error on out‑of‑distribution corner cases was 2.3 %, confirming that the surrogate fidelity is not the limiting factor in the insulation design.

### 8.4 Tier 4 — Controller Verification Plan

The trained SAC policy will be evaluated on the full‑order CFD twin (Star‑CCM+ coupled with the lumped‑parameter thermal models) under a battery of randomised scenarios, including extreme heat‑flux spikes, sensor failures, and slosh disturbances. The deterministic safety shield is analytically proven (Appendix A.4) to prevent over‑pressurisation in all cases, and this guarantee will be confirmed numerically. The projected 22 % cumulative reward advantage over the linearised MPC baseline, anticipated from the structural pre‑cooling capability of the RL agent, will be verified or refuted during this campaign. A comprehensive ablation study over the reward weights will be conducted to ensure that the policy’s qualitative behaviour is robust. No part of this controller verification has been performed; the plan described here constitutes the immediate next step in the research programme.

### 8.5 Residual Uncertainties and Risk Mitigation

The most significant residual uncertainty in the validation chain is the extrapolation from the PnP simulant to the mission fluids LCH₄ and LOX. The combined effect of the accommodation coefficient, surface tension gradient, and thermal diffusivity differences is estimated to introduce an additional \(\pm 12\%\) to the pressure‑rise prediction, bringing the expected total twin accuracy to approximately \(\pm 12\%\) for the mission fluids. This uncertainty is adequately covered by the \(\pm 30\%\) heat‑flux randomisation envelope proposed for RL training. Sub‑scale LCH₄/LOX calorimetry testing, in which a small instrumented tank is subjected to controlled heat loads with helium pressurant, is recommended as the highest‑priority ground‑test activity to reduce this uncertainty before flight qualification.

