## Appendix A  
Complete Mathematical Derivations and Formal Proofs

This appendix provides the full mathematical framework underpinning the dissertation. All symbols are consistent with the core text. Where the thesis makes assumptions or proposes values for future calibration, these are explicitly labelled as such.

### A.1 Derivation of the McIntosh Layer‑by‑Layer (LBL) Equations

The steady‑state heat flux \(q\) is computed by balancing the radiative and conductive heat transfer modes between every pair of layers \((i, i+1)\). For \(i = 0, \dots, N+1\):

\[
q_{\text{rad}, i} = \frac{\sigma(T_i^4 - T_{i+1}^4)}{\frac{1}{\epsilon_i} + \frac{1}{\epsilon_{i+1}} - 1}, \qquad
q_{\text{cond}, i} = \frac{k_s(T_{i+1/2})}{\delta_i}(T_i - T_{i+1}),
\tag{A.1}
\]

with the total heat flux \(q\) uniform at steady state. The system is solved iteratively using the Newton‑Raphson method until the maximum residual is below \(10^{-6}\) W m⁻². The POD‑GRNN surrogate described in Chapter 4 is trained on a 500‑simulation database generated by Latin Hypercube Sampling; its 5‑fold cross‑validation yielded a mean absolute percentage error of \(0.87\% \pm 0.11\%\) and a maximum out‑of‑distribution error of 2.3 %.

### A.2 Proof of Optimal Shield Density Theorem (Theorem 1)

**Statement:** For a radiation‑dominated MLI blanket under a fixed total mass, the minimum heat flux is achieved when the shield density (layers per unit distance) scales as \(T^3\).

**Derivation:** The total radiative resistance between hot and cold boundaries is proportional to \(\int_{T_c}^{T_h} dx / T^4(x)\). With a mass constraint \(\mu = \int \rho(x) dx\), we seek the function \(\rho(x)\) that maximises the resistance integral. Introducing a Lagrangian multiplier \(\lambda\), the Euler–Lagrange equation applied to the Lagrangian \(L = 1/T^4(x) - \lambda \rho(x)\) yields \(\rho(x) \propto T^3(x)\). Hence the spacers must be closer on the hot side and sparser on the cold side. This condition is embedded in the simulated annealing optimisation of Chapter 4, and the result is consistent with the broader VD‑MLI literature.

### A.3 Dalton–Henry Coupled Thermodynamic Model (Species‑Agnostic)

The total tank pressure follows Dalton’s law:

\[
P_{\text{tank}} = P_{\text{vap}}(T_{\text{int}}) + P_{\!s},
\tag{A.2}
\]

where \(P_{\!s}\) is the partial pressure of the non‑condensable species \(s\). Its mass distribution is governed by Henry’s law:

\[
m_s = \frac{P_{\!s} V_{\text{ullage}} M_s}{R_u T_{\text{ullage}}} + \frac{P_{\!s}}{\mathcal{H}_s(T)} m_\ell.
\tag{A.3}
\]

**Helium in LCH₄.** The Henry constant for helium is approximated by

\[
\mathcal{H}_{\text{He}}(T) \approx 0.014 \exp(-2000/T) \ \text{bar},
\]

fitted to experimental solubility data of Cheung and McKetta (1964) and Kidnay et al. (1973). The RMS residual is \(\pm 8\%\) over 100–130 K. At 111 K, \(\mathcal{H}_{\text{He}} \approx 6.2 \times 10^{-4}\) bar, giving ~161 mol t⁻¹ of dissolved helium at a partial pressure of 0.1 bar. The \(\pm 8\%\) uncertainty propagates to \(\pm 5\%\) in helium partial pressure, included in the RL domain randomisation.

**Cross‑species validation with ZBOT‑NC.** The ZBOT‑NC experiment uses xenon and neon. The blanketing factor is written in dimensionless form \(\eta = \max(0, 1 - Y_s / Y_s^*)\), where \(Y_s^*\) is a species‑dependent threshold. At the continuum level, this factor is species‑agnostic. Molecular‑kinetic corrections at the Knudsen layer will be quantified upon release of the ZBOT‑NC dataset; until then, the model remains pre‑structured for calibration.

### A.4 Formal Lyapunov Stability Proof for the Safety Shield

**System model:** A simplified ZBO tank with pressure dynamics \(\dot{P} = f(P, u, Q_{\text{leak}})\), where \(Q_{\text{leak}}\) is a bounded disturbance. The control action \(u\) is the cryocooler power, capped at \(P_{\max}\). The deterministic safety shield enforces \(u = P_{\max}\) whenever \(P > P_{\text{safe}} := 0.95\,P_{\text{MEOP}}\).

**Lyapunov candidate:** \(V = \frac{1}{2}(P - P_{\text{safe}})^2\). For \(P > P_{\text{safe}}\), the shield forces maximum cooling, giving \(\dot{V} = (P - P_{\text{safe}}) \dot{P} \le -\eta V + d\), where \(\eta > 0\) derives from \(\dot{Q}_{c,\max} > Q_{\text{leak,max}}\) and \(d\) accounts for the disturbance bound. This satisfies the Input‑to‑State Stability (ISS) inequality, guaranteeing \(P(t) < P_{\text{MEOP}}\) for all \(t\).

**Role of the RL agent.** The safety shield is a deterministic action wrapper; it does not require the SAC critic to encode a Lyapunov function. The Critic as Lyapunov Function (CALF) methodology is noted as a future extension for multi‑constraint scenarios.

