## Appendix C  
Final Algorithm — Lyapunov‑Shielded Soft Actor‑Critic (LS‑SAC) for ZBO Pressure Control

This appendix presents the planned RL control algorithm. No training has been executed; the description constitutes the ready‑to‑implement blueprint.

### C.1 Algorithm Pseudocode

**Algorithm:** Lyapunov‑Shielded SAC for ZBO Pressure Control

```
Initialize:
  Replay buffer D (size 2×10⁶)
  SAC policy parameters ϕ, twin Q‑function parameters θ₁, θ₂
  Target network weights θ̄₁ ← θ₁, θ̄₂ ← θ₂
  Safety threshold P_safe = 0.95 × MEOP

Loop for each training episode (planned: 1 million episodes):
  for t = 1 to T:
    Observe state s_t (14‑dimensional, see below)
    Compute safe action: u_safe = P_max if s_t.P > P_safe else None
    Sample action a_t ∼ π_ϕ(·|s_t)
    Apply a_final = min(a_t, u_safe) if shield active else a_t
    Observe next state s_{t+1} and reward r_t (Equation 7.1)
    Store (s_t, a_t, r_t, s_{t+1}) in D

  Sample minibatch of N transitions from D
  Compute target value y_i as per SAC standard (Equation A.10 of main text)
  Update Q‑functions by minimizing soft Bellman residual
  Update policy by minimizing SAC loss (Equation A.11)
  Adjust entropy temperature α
  Soft‑update target networks: θ̄_i ← τθ_i + (1-τ)θ̄_i

  Every 10,000 timesteps:
    Evaluate policy on the digital twin (planned full‑order CFD)
    Log pressure overshoot, vent mass, power consumption
```

After training, the policy will be distilled into a compact feed‑forward network (3 layers, 128 neurons each) for flight‑processor embedding.

### C.2 Reward Function and Planned Ablation

\[
r(s_t, a_t) = -w_1 \dot{m}_{\text{vent}}^2 - w_2 a_t + w_3 \mathbb{1}_{P < P_{\text{set}}},
\tag{7.1}
\]

with nominal weights \(w_1 = 10^{-8}\), \(w_2 = 10^{-3}\), \(w_3 = 0.5\). These are chosen so that all terms are dimensionally comparable at the expected vent rate (~10⁻⁴ kg s⁻¹) and cooler power (0–380 W). A planned ablation study will test three alternative weight sets—strong energy penalty, removal of the pressure‑bonus term, and heavy vent penalty—to verify that the policy’s anticipatory behaviour is robust. The target performance metric is a ≥22 % cumulative reward advantage over a linearised MPC under a 30 % heat‑flux spike; the actual figure will be determined once training is complete.

### C.3 State Vector and Domain Randomisation

**14‑dimensional state:** \(s = [P, T_\ell, T_v, P_{\text{He}}, \dot{Q}_{\text{solar}}, P_{\text{elec}}, T_{\text{cold}}, t_{\text{event}}, \nabla T_{\ell,1}, \dots, \nabla T_{\ell,5}]\).

**Domain randomisation (planned):** Heat flux \(\pm 30\%\), sensor noise \(\pm 20\%\), random slosh events, accommodation coefficient uncertainty (equivalent to \(\pm 18\%\) pressure rise), Henry constant uncertainty (\(\pm 5\%\) partial pressure). The safety shield proof is independent of these parameters, guaranteeing pressure containment throughout.

