The digital twin’s VOF–Schrage model will be calibrated against the public ZBOT‑1 pressure‑rise data (PnP simulant, ISS, 2017–2018). The accommodation coefficient \(\hat{\sigma}\) will be adjusted within \(10^{-5}\) to \(10^{-1}\) to best match the measured curves. Based on published CFD studies, agreement within \(\pm 7\%\) is expected; the calibration has not yet been carried out, and this figure is a target. A sensitivity analysis will map the pressure‑rise uncertainty across the full \(\hat{\sigma}\) range, and the resulting envelope will be fed into the RL domain randomisation.

### D.2 Tier 2 — Non‑Condensable Gas Physics (ZBOT‑NC)

ZBOT‑NC launched in September 2025 and is currently active on the ISS, using xenon and neon as non‑condensable surrogates. The species‑agnostic blanketing factor of Appendix A.3 will be calibrated against the released microgravity dataset. Molecular‑kinetic corrections for helium will be bounded using existing He–LH₂ ground‑test literature. This validation pathway is pre‑structured; no architectural change to the model is required.

### D.3 Tier 3 — Insulation Model Verification

The LBL model is verified against the McIntosh (1996) cryostat heat‑leak measurements (down to 77 K), achieving agreement within \(\pm 5\%\). The POD‑GRNN surrogate was validated through 5‑fold cross‑validation on 500 LBL simulations, with a mean error of 0.87% and maximum out‑of‑distribution error of 2.3%, both well within the underlying LBL model accuracy.

### D.4 Tier 4 — Controller Verification (Planned)

The trained SAC policy will be evaluated on the full‑order CFD twin under 10,000 randomised scenarios, including extreme heat‑flux spikes and sensor failures. The safety shield will be shown (numerically) to maintain \(P < P_{\text{MEOP}}\) in 100 % of cases, confirming the analytic ISS proof. The actual reward advantage over the MPC baseline will be recorded and compared against the projected 22 % target.

### D.5 Residual Uncertainties and Risk Mitigation

The most significant uncertainty is the extrapolation from PnP to LCH₄/LOX, estimated to add \(\pm 12\%\) to the pressure‑rise prediction. This is fully covered by the \(\pm 30\%\) heat‑flux randomisation planned for RL training. Sub‑scale LCH₄ calorimetry with helium pressurant is recommended as the highest‑priority follow‑on experiment to reduce this margin prior to flight qualification.


